[Event "Winning paths and distant opposition"]
[ChapterName "Black to move: take the opposition"]
[Orientation "black"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/3k4/8/8/3PK3/8 b - - 0 1"]

{ [%ts 10:55] The same starting position, king on d5 against king and pawn on d2, but Black to move this time. You're Black. What does the opposition tell you? }
1... Ke4 $1 { [%ts 11:03] King e4. Now Black has the opposition and stops the white king from coming up. }
( 1... Kd4 { Also holds by the engine. John takes the opposition with Ke4. } )
( 1... Kd6 { Also holds by the engine. John takes the opposition with Ke4. } )
( 1... Ke6 { Also holds by the engine. John takes the opposition with Ke4. } )
2. d3+
2... Kd4 $1
( 2... Kd5 { Also holds by the engine. John steps to d4, in front of the pawn. } )
( 2... Ke5 { Also holds by the engine. John steps to d4, in front of the pawn. } )
( 2... Kf5 { Also holds by the engine. John steps to d4, in front of the pawn. } )
( 2... Kf4 { Also holds by the engine. John steps to d4, in front of the pawn. } )
3. Kd2 { [%ts 11:19] Kd2. Don't panic: having to retreat is no big deal, because the pawn coming forward doesn't mean White wins. }
3... Kd5 $1 { [%ts 11:35] When you have to retreat, go directly backwards. It keeps both e5 and c5 ready, depending on which way the white king goes. }
( 3... Ke5 { [%ts 11:35] King e5 doesn't spoil anything for now, but going straight back is the safest rule. } )
( 3... Kc5 { [%ts 11:35] King c5 doesn't spoil anything for now, but going straight back is the safest rule. } )
4. Ke3
4... Ke5 $1 { [%ts 11:52] King e5: the opposition again. }
5. d4+
5... Kd5 $1 { [%ts 12:00] Right back in front of the pawn. }
( 5... Kd6 { Also holds by the engine. John goes right back in front of the pawn, to d5. } )
( 5... Ke6 { Also holds by the engine. John goes right back in front of the pawn, to d5. } )
( 5... Kf5 { Also holds by the engine. John goes right back in front of the pawn, to d5. } )
( 5... Kf6 { Also holds by the engine. John goes right back in front of the pawn, to d5. } )
6. Kd3 { [%ts 12:00] White technically has the opposition, but there's a pawn in the way. }
6... Kd6 $1 { [%ts 12:08] King d6. White can't keep the opposition: he goes to e4 or c4, and Black has a matching square. }
( 6... Ke6 { Also holds by the engine. John steps straight back, to d6. } )
( 6... Kc6 { Also holds by the engine. John steps straight back, to d6. } )
7. Ke4 { [%ts 12:08] White goes to e4. Find the matching square. }
7... Ke6 $1 { [%ts 12:16] [%cal Ge6e4] The defender likes symmetry: kings on the same file, so the white king can't come up. It's a dead draw. }
