[Event "Winning paths and distant opposition"]
[ChapterName "Black's only move"]
[Orientation "black"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/3k4/8/8/8/3PK3/8 b - - 0 1"]

{ [%ts 13:08] Change one thing: the black king starts on d6. With White to move it's a draw, because Kd3 meets ...Kd5. With Black to move it's harder: Black has to find an only move. You're Black. }
1... Ke6 $1 { [%ts 16:14] [%cal Ge6e2] King e6: the opposition. Same colour squares on the e-file, an odd number of squares between the kings (three this time, distant opposition), and White to move. Whoever breaks the standoff first loses it. }
( 1... Kd5 $2 { [%ts 15:25] The natural move loses: Kd3 puts Black in zugzwang. Black has to give ground and Kc4 outflanks. } )
( 1... Ke5 $2 { [%ts 15:57] White plays Ke3, and it's the same conclusion: Black has to give ground. } )
( 1... Kc5 $2 { [%ts 17:03] Going to the c-file allows Ke3. The defender shouldn't get into an outflanking race: keep the kings lined up on the file. } )
2. Kd3
2... Kd5 $1 { [%ts 14:02] Kd5, and Black has taken the opposition. Wherever the white king goes, Black follows. }
3. Ke3 { [%ts 14:10] Whichever way the white king goes, the defender follows along the file. }
3... Ke5 $1 { [%ts 14:36] King e5, staying in front of the king along the file. }
( 3... Kc4 $2 { [%ts 14:10] Don't outflank as the defender: White plays Ke4 and you're losing. } )
4. Kf3 { [%ts 14:19] If White goes too far from the pawn, like Kf3... }
4... Kd4 $1 { [%ts 14:19] ...cut back towards the pawn and stay right in front of it. }
( 4... Kd5 { Also holds by the engine. John cuts back towards the pawn, in front of it. } )
( 4... Kf5 { Also holds by the engine. John cuts back towards the pawn, in front of it. } )
