[Event "Winning paths and distant opposition"]
[ChapterName "The reserve tempo"]
[Orientation "white"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/3k4/8/3K4/8/3P4/8 w - - 0 1"]

{ [%ts 18:17] An exception. The black king started way back on d8 and took the distant opposition with ...Ke8, five squares between the kings. Then both kings came up: White to d4, Black to d6. It looks like the standoff from the last chapter. What should White do? }
1. d3 $1 { [%ts 18:46] [%cal Gd2d3] d3, a reserve tempo. White throws the move back to Black: the king stays on d4, and now Black has to move his. }
( 1. Ke4 { Also wins by the engine, since the pawn keeps its spare move for later. John spends it now: find the pawn move. } )
( 1. Kc4 { Also wins by the engine, since the pawn keeps its spare move for later. John spends it now: find the pawn move. } )
1... Ke6 { [%ts 19:03] The opposition depends on whose move it is, so Black isn't happy. Say ...Ke6. }
( 1... Kc6 { [%ts 19:11] If Black leaves d6 the other way, to c6, outflank on the other side. } 2. Ke5 $1 { [%ts 19:11] Ke5: the mirror image of Kc5 against ...Ke6. } ( 2. Ke4 { Also wins by the engine, but it gains no ground. Outflank. } ) ( 2. Kc4 { Also wins by the engine, but it gains no ground. Outflank. } ) )
2. Kc5 $1 { [%ts 19:11] Kc5, outflanking, and White will win. }
( 2. Ke4 { Also wins by the engine, but it gains no ground. Outflank. } ) ( 2. Kc4 { Also wins by the engine, but it gains no ground. Outflank. } ) 
